1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
You've probably heard all the hype

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:05,520
around second brains and Claude OS.

3
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:07,600
People have been using Claude code to

4
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:09,480
set up their entire systems, running it

5
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,480
like an operating system instead of just

6
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:13,160
a coding agent. But these systems come

7
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:15,080
with their own problems. When someone

8
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:17,040
sets up a second brain, they build it

9
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:18,880
for their own use and structure it the

10
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:20,600
way they think works best. There's no

11
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:22,680
standard way of doing it, which makes

12
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:24,400
these systems hard to navigate and keeps

13
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:26,160
them from being shareable. To solve

14
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:28,120
this, Google just released the open

15
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:30,160
knowledge format, where Google is giving

16
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:32,320
their own way on how to build such

17
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:34,160
operating systems with AI. If this is

18
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:36,440
your first time here, we're a software

19
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:38,160
company and this is our channel AI labs,

20
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:40,400
where we show you how to optimize your

21
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:42,240
own processes with AI the same way we've

22
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,120
done with ours. And in this video, we're

23
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:46,080
going to tell you what this format is,

24
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:48,440
how it solves the problem, and why it

25
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:49,880
actually matters for your workflows. But

26
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:51,680
before [snorts] we get into what Google

27
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:53,320
did, let's first understand the real

28
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:55,520
issue. In our previous videos, we've

29
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:57,360
talked about maintaining a second brain,

30
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:58,960
and we manage one for ourselves, too,

31
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:00,960
where we keep all our strategies,

32
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:02,960
research, and guidelines. It's version

33
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:04,760
controlled with Git and push to GitHub,

34
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:06,680
and everyone on our team has access to

35
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:08,640
it. So whenever new people join us, they

36
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:10,280
can just pull it and get context on how

37
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:12,080
we work. And like we mentioned before,

38
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:14,520
this second brain is controlled by a

39
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:16,440
Claude.md file. That file basically

40
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:17,960
guides the agent on how to navigate

41
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:20,200
around the brain. We've also got

42
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:21,960
dedicated Claude.md files in each

43
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:23,400
folder, so the agent has specific

44
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:25,080
instructions for working in that

45
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:26,640
directory. But even though Claude is

46
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:28,560
pretty good at getting context from

47
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:30,760
files, it still messes up a lot. It

48
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:32,480
happened to us so many times would put a

49
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:34,080
file in the wrong place, and then we'd

50
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:35,680
have to remind it where it actually

51
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:37,360
goes. And after that, it would just

52
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:39,200
create a new folder for it, simply

53
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:41,160
because it doesn't know similar info

54
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:43,120
already exists in another folder under a

55
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:45,080
different name. The real problem is that

56
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:47,080
Claude doesn't know the info it needs

57
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:48,880
already exists in the knowledge base. It

58
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:50,720
only finds things when it actively

59
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:52,640
searches for them. So unless you tell it

60
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:54,360
to look in a certain file, it won't even

61
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:55,960
know that file is there. This isn't

62
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:58,080
really obvious in smaller knowledge

63
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:59,600
bases, but it becomes a lot more visible

64
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:01,400
once you're working with a big one. The

65
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:03,360
way Claude searches is by matching

66
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:05,560
keywords against the file content, and

67
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:07,240
it uses the file names as a guide, too.

68
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:09,399
So, if you ask it to search through a

69
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:11,120
really nested folder structure, it has

70
00:02:09,399 --> 00:02:12,920
to make a bunch of attempts before it

71
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:14,800
lands on the right file. This not only

72
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:17,080
wastes time, but it consumes a lot of

73
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:18,840
tokens as well. So, Google just launched

74
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,959
open knowledge format, and the problem

75
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:22,440
it fixes is standardization. And this is

76
00:02:20,959 --> 00:02:24,360
something we've already seen happen

77
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:26,240
across agents a bunch of times. When

78
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:28,280
there was a need to let agents talk to

79
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:30,560
external resources beyond what they had

80
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:32,320
in the terminal, they introduced MCPs,

81
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,959
and it became a protocol that every

82
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:35,920
agent adopted. In the same way,

83
00:02:33,959 --> 00:02:38,400
packaging reusable instructions came in

84
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:40,360
the form of skills, and just like MCPs,

85
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:42,120
they spread across every agent. And when

86
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:43,920
there was a need to standardize how you

87
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:46,240
communicate design intent, Google

88
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:47,800
launched the design.md standard, too.

89
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:49,600
So, just like there's always a need to

90
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:51,400
standardize things, there was a need to

91
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:53,520
standardize knowledge, too. And that's

92
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,440
exactly what open knowledge format does.

93
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:57,480
Now, this idea isn't really new. It's

94
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:59,280
based on the LLM Wiki pattern which

95
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:01,040
Andrej Karpathy came up with, and it got

96
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:02,680
really popular a while ago. Before he

97
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:04,440
came up with this, people were relying

98
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:06,440
on the rag approach where you convert

99
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:08,519
all your huge documents into vector

100
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:10,480
format. And vectors do help because they

101
00:03:08,519 --> 00:03:12,440
basically put everything into a form

102
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:14,120
that models can understand. From there,

103
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:16,160
the system matches the meaning of your

104
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:18,239
query against the existing data and

105
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:20,000
returns the most relevant matches. But

106
00:03:18,239 --> 00:03:21,959
Karpathy pointed out that this causes

107
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,600
issues. Whenever you ask a question, the

108
00:03:21,959 --> 00:03:25,440
agent is basically rebuilding the

109
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:27,120
information from scratch. It hands you

110
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:28,959
an answer, but it's not building up any

111
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:30,959
knowledge over time. So, he suggested

112
00:03:28,959 --> 00:03:33,000
using markdown files to build knowledge

113
00:03:30,959 --> 00:03:35,120
bases instead because that way the agent

114
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,160
can actually gather context as it goes.

115
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:39,160
His approach used in models' ability to

116
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:41,280
navigate a file system. And after he

117
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:43,160
shared the idea, a lot of people started

118
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:44,880
building second brains of their own. But

119
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:47,000
the problem was that each one was

120
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:48,840
designed around its creator's personal

121
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,880
workflow. The person who organized it

122
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:52,920
knew what was in each folder and could

123
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:54,600
navigate it easily with the agent. But a

124
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:56,160
new person would have a hard time

125
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:58,080
because they'd have to spend time

126
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:59,880
letting the agent explore the folders

127
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,280
and figure out what the knowledge base

128
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:04,280
actually holds. OKP solves this by

129
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:06,240
creating a standard way of organizing

130
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:08,360
files so that not just an agent but a

131
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:10,120
human can also understand what's inside

132
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:12,240
the knowledge base. It makes knowledge

133
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:14,000
shareable by packaging it into a bundle.

134
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:16,079
And this bundle can contain markdown

135
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,440
files which hold the actual information

136
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:19,320
about whatever you're building the

137
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:21,560
knowledge base for. Each file also

138
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:22,960
includes YAML front matter, which is

139
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:24,960
basically a small block at the top of

140
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:26,919
the file that describes what's inside it

141
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:29,080
so the agent knows what that file holds.

142
00:04:26,919 --> 00:04:30,880
So OKP doesn't really introduce anything

143
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:33,000
new. Instead, it gives you a standard

144
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:34,840
format that anyone can produce and read

145
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,640
and it makes knowledge portable across

146
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:38,080
different systems. When we first heard

147
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:39,919
about it and went through it, one

148
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:41,880
thought came to mind. Since Google is

149
00:04:39,919 --> 00:04:43,720
trying to turn web search into agentic

150
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:45,640
search, this could also be an attempt to

151
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:47,640
support that shift. Right now, websites

152
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:49,440
are adding LLMs.txt

153
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:51,160
files because they hold information

154
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:53,040
about the website that's specifically

155
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:55,280
tailored for models and that gives those

156
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:58,040
agentic systems context about the site.

157
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:59,919
So instead of relying only on LLMs.txt,

158
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:02,360
websites might eventually start adding

159
00:04:59,919 --> 00:05:04,400
OKP bundles, too. That would let agents

160
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:06,240
query their content more efficiently and

161
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:08,240
maybe give better search results based

162
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:10,240
on that structured info. Right now, it's

163
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:12,200
only meant for internal use, but this is

164
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:13,640
something that could end up happening.

165
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:15,919
So to [snorts] understand how it helps,

166
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:17,600
let's see how OKP works under the hood.

167
00:05:15,919 --> 00:05:19,040
This system takes everything that's a

168
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:20,720
part of your knowledge base and

169
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:22,640
represents it as objects called

170
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:24,680
concepts. It could be your data,

171
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:26,480
markdown documents, YAML files, or

172
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:28,200
literally anything else that goes into

173
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:29,880
it. The structure works like this. All

174
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:31,960
the information you want to organize

175
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:33,680
gets placed inside folders named after

176
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:35,680
the topic and each one only holds

177
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:38,520
content about that one topic. And within

178
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:40,000
every folder, there's an index.md file.

179
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:41,640
This one's the most important because

180
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,640
it's what the agent reads first. It

181
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:45,640
gives the agent context on what's inside

182
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:47,760
that folder. Each concept document has a

183
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:49,480
small YAML block that includes a name

184
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:51,120
and a description. That lets the agent

185
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:52,760
know exactly what it is and what's

186
00:05:51,120 --> 00:05:54,680
inside the document. And just like

187
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:56,920
skills have a similar YAML block, this

188
00:05:54,680 --> 00:05:59,040
serves the exact same purpose. It feeds

189
00:05:56,920 --> 00:06:00,920
the agent context bit by bit, so it only

190
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:02,640
loads the exact thing it needs by

191
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:04,360
reading these descriptions first and

192
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:06,320
then pulling in the relevant content.

193
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:08,000
The main principle OKF is built on is

194
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:10,040
minimalism. The idea is that each

195
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,880
concept should represent only one thing,

196
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:13,440
and the type field inside the document

197
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:15,160
tells you what that thing is. It

198
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:16,760
shouldn't hold multiple unrelated

199
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:18,560
things. Because the moment a concept

200
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:20,800
mixes topics, the agent loses the

201
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:23,000
ability to load the exact information it

202
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:24,640
needs. Another principle of OKF is

203
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,480
separating the knowledge base from

204
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:28,480
whoever's consuming it. Whether it's an

205
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:30,080
agent, a human, a team member, or

206
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:32,240
anything else, the knowledge itself

207
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:34,080
stays independent. It's not also tied to

208
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:35,680
any specific platform, which is what

209
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:37,520
makes it usable with pretty much

210
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:39,120
anything. But before we see this system

211
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:41,120
in action, let's have a word by our

212
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:43,600
sponsor, Mobbin. If you've used tools

213
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:45,720
like Cursor, Lovable, or Claude Code to

214
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:47,600
build a UI, you've probably noticed they

215
00:06:45,720 --> 00:06:49,760
all spit out the same thing, the same

216
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:51,800
hero section, card layout, and same

217
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:53,560
generic onboarding. It looks like AI

218
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:55,520
slop, and the reason is simple. These

219
00:06:53,560 --> 00:06:57,480
tools have never actually seen what good

220
00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:59,960
design looks like, but Mobbin has.

221
00:06:57,480 --> 00:07:02,120
Mobbin just launched an MCP server that

222
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:04,840
connects your AI tools directly to their

223
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:07,600
library of over 621,000

224
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:09,320
real app screens and 142,000

225
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:11,640
flows from shipped products like

226
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:13,400
Revolut, Uber, and Wise. So, here's how

227
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:15,400
I used it. I was building a checkout

228
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:17,320
flow and asked my agent to reference how

229
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:19,320
the best apps handle it. The key part,

230
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:21,560
it's not copying screens. Mobbin gives

231
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:23,720
the agent the real flows, states, and

232
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:25,480
hierarchy behind those designs before it

233
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:27,320
writes any code, so it builds from

234
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:29,200
proven patterns instead of guessing.

235
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:31,760
Setup takes under a minute, and it works

236
00:07:29,200 --> 00:07:34,120
across Claude, Cursor, V0, and more. Try

237
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:36,480
Mobbin MCP using the link in the pinned

238
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:38,280
comment. So, we wanted to see how this

239
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:39,760
system actually performs in a real

240
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:41,680
setup. And since we were already

241
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:43,400
maintaining a second brain that's shared

242
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:45,560
across our team through GitHub, we

243
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:47,360
tested OKF on it. But we didn't want to

244
00:07:45,560 --> 00:07:49,120
touch the main branch in case it didn't

245
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:50,840
work out. So we created a new branch,

246
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:52,600
which is basically a separate copy of

247
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:55,280
the project, and made all our changes

248
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:57,240
there. So OKF basically ships with three

249
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:59,040
things. The first one is an enrichment

250
00:07:57,240 --> 00:08:01,040
agent. It takes the data that's sitting

251
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:03,400
in BigQuery, which is basically Google's

252
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:05,680
big database for storing data, converts

253
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:07,760
it into OKF concept documents, and then

254
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:10,160
runs an LLM pass to check them. Then

255
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:12,600
there's an HTML visualization tool that

256
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:14,640
turns an OKF bundle into an interactive

257
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:16,640
graph view that's easier to explore. And

258
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:18,800
it comes with examples of what properly

259
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:20,880
formatted OKF data should look like,

260
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:22,280
which the agent can use as a reference.

261
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:24,000
Now, since we weren't working with

262
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:25,640
BigQuery, we didn't need that first

263
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,800
part. It would have needed setting up a

264
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:29,560
whole project around it on Google Cloud,

265
00:08:27,800 --> 00:08:31,280
which we didn't need since our project

266
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:33,360
was already tracked with Git. But the

267
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:35,360
tool it ships with for turning data into

268
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:37,479
the OKF format is designed only for

269
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:39,599
BigQuery. So as a workaround, we created

270
00:08:37,479 --> 00:08:41,680
a skill called markdown to OKF. What

271
00:08:39,599 --> 00:08:43,680
this skill does is convert any folder of

